ll of pieces on row C and some scattered presence on rows F and G. The bottom edge (Row H) is split by an opponent\'s piece at H2. My goal is to consolidate my territory and limit the opponent\'s mobility.\n\nEvaluation:\n1.  **Move (H, 3)**: This is a very strong move on the bottom edge. By placing a piece at H3, I flank and flip the opponent\'s piece at H2 using my corner at H1. Since I also own the other corner at H8, removing the opponent from this row is vital. It creates a connected line of stable pieces (H1-H2-H3) and prevents the opponent from using H2 to disrupt my edge control.\n2.  **Move (F, 1)**: This is another solid edge move. It uses my corner at H1 to flip the opponent\'s piece at G1, extending my control up the left column. While good, the left column is not capped by a corner at the top (A1 is empty), making it slightly less valuable than the bottom row where I own both ends.\n3.  **Move (B, 4)**: This move would flip pieces in Row C (C3, C4, C5). It blocks the opponent from making a potentially strong move at G4 (which would flip my center pieces). However, playing in Row B opens up the game and is less "quiet" than the edge moves.\n\nReason:\nThe strategy in Reversi heavily favors securing "stable" discs—pieces that cannot be flipped back. Owning both corners of an edge (H1 and H8) presents a golden opportunity to make the entire edge stable. Move (H, 3) is the direct step to achieve this by removing the only opponent piece (H2) on that edge. This solidifies my advantage significantly more than fighting in the center or extending up an uncapped edge.\n\nAnswer: (H, 3)'}
